in this lesson we're going to practice  naming alkenes and alkynes I put a link  in the video description to blank copies  of these problems so you could practice  by solving them first and then watch the  video if you'd like let's get started  with a problem that involves an alkene  and alkyne in the same molecule as  always with nomenclature problems we  need to start by identifying our longest  carbon chain if we count from this end 1  2 3 4 5 and if we count from up here  it'll be the same 1 2 3 4 5 so we can  circle either this or this chain here  now we need to figure out which end to  number from this end or this end well we  want to use the end where we hit the  multiple bond first so we're going to  start numbering here on the right okay  now let's put together our suffix at the  four position we have a group hanging  off  that's a methyl so we're going to begin  this name with four methyl now we need  to name our chain it has 5 carbons so it  would be a pentane we have an alkene and  alkyne in it so it's actually going to  be a Penta nine I stopped at pent here  because now we actually need to define  the position within the chain where we  find our alkyne since we're naming this  as in a nine the alkyne begins at the 3  carbon so we need to add in a number 3  before we define our alkyne now if there  just were an alkyne this would be een  but since we're continuing the name we  have to drop that final e to tell where  our alkyne is our alkyne is at the 1  position so actually we can completely  leave it out and just write een ein or  we can define it a little bit better  using the number one because the alkyne  begins at number one in the chain  putting it all together this compound is  for methyl pent 3 een 1/9 let's do  another a nine example here we've added  on just an extra methyl group so we know  that's lengthened our chain by one and  we can circle it in the same way  as we did on this example now we need to  figure out which end to number from if  we look at this we see if we numbered  here the alkene would be at the two  position one two oh and the alkyne would  also be at a two position so when we're  given this choice we pick the alkene to  number first so in this case we're going  to number from left to right let's go  ahead and add in our prefix okay now we  have six carbons in our chain  so that's hexane for the parent alkane  so let's write on our hex now even  though the numbering priority favored  the alkene the suffix is set up the same  so this is still in a nine now our  alkene starts on the number two and our  alkyne begins on number four putting  that all together we get two methyl hex  two een four ein ok let's try a cyclic  example here we have one two three four  five six carbons within the ring and we  want to number so that this bromine atom  is number one now we need to figure out  if we number clockwise or in the  counterclockwise direction the answer  here is clockwise because we want to  number each end of the alkyne first so  we continue along this alkyne the second  position gets the two and then we can  finish numbering our ring for six carbon  atoms the parent alkane would be  cyclohexane so we're going to use the  hex name in our suffix what about our  prefix well we have one substituent at  the one position so we'll write in one  bromo cyclo hexa start now in this  example we have a dying we have two  alkenes within this molecule and so we  would usually drop the a but in this one  since it's going to be followed by dying  we'll put cyclo hexa now we needed to  find where these two alkenes are within  our ring this one starts at the one  position and this one begins at the four  position so we'll add in those numbers  into our name here  the final part of our name is dying to  account for our two double bonds let's  try a few more alkenes and practice  double bond geometry here's our example  our longest carbon chain is kind of  obvious going just right across here now  which end do we start numbering from we  want to number so that we hit the double  bond first so we'll start on the left  now to start our prefix bromine B comes  in the alphabet before C for chlorine so  we'll start with bromine I went ahead  netted the chlorine in there too now we  need to specify where our double bond  begins and that's on carbon 2 so we can  add on the suffix 2 n but there's  something special about this molecule  that we haven't talked about yet here  there are two different groups on this  side of the double bond and here there  are also two different groups on this  side on carbon 2 we have chlorine and a  methyl and on carbon three we have  methyl and a hydrogen atom that's  implied that I've not drawn in in our  first example we had the same group on  carbon 2 we had two methyls but the  chlorine here could be on this side of  the bond or this side of the bond and  that would make a different molecule so  we actually need to define where  chlorine is so taking both sides of the  double bond we want to figure out if the  high priority groups are on the same  side which would be a z alkene or on  opposite sides which would be an e  alkene to do this we pick the group with  the higher molecular mass so on carbon 2  this is going to be chlorine now on this  side on carbon 3 we have a hydrogen atom  the lowest mass compound that we can  have and a carbon so this side is our  higher priority group these two groups  are on the same side of the alkene so  this is a z alkene that designation goes  right out in front and is very important  so don't forget it  here's another example  obviously our longest carbon chain is  here I'll go ahead and circle that let's  number our carbons starting near the end  with the double bond nine carbons makes  this a non mean let's write up our  prefix first chlorine is at the eighth  position so eight chloro and then we  have a non - to define where the double  bond starts okay now we need to think  about our double bond geometry let me  add in the hydrogen atoms so on either  side the choices between hydrogen and  carbon here at carbon two we have a  hydrogen atom and a methyl the carbon  atom is going to have the higher  priority and on carbon three right here  we have an H and a carbon atom and so  this group over here gets higher  priority we can see from our stars that  the higher priority groups are on  opposite sides of the alkene now if this  is an alkene so we'll put that right out  in front  I was once taught a funny trick for  remembering double bond geometry for the  Z alkene the high priority groups are on  the same side so you can say on the same  side to remember this let's do a final  example okay at first glance this looks  almost identical to this problem but  it's in fact very different when there's  an alcohol in the molecule it takes  priority over the naming of the alkene  in both numbering and in the suffix and  so this compound is named as an enol and  we start numbering from the side closer  to the alcohol we again have an e double  bond so let's start with that then we  get our nonane part of the name the  alkene begins on the seven position now  and the alcohol had priority and it's  the last part of our name that's at the  two position okay let's summarize the  major takeaways from this lesson  numbering begins at the side of the  molecule that first hits the alkene or  the L kind but if you have a choice you  number so that the alkene gets the lower  number  when numbering in rings make sure you  hit both carbons of the double-bond  first and then continue around your ring  if we think about splitting the double  bond in half when there are different  groups on either side of this double  bond we need to think about Z and a  geometry finally we saw that alcohols  have priority over alkenes in both  numbering and in the suffix KP here if  you learn something give me a thumbs up  on the way out and for more chemistry  subscribe to my channel
 

